#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105691 :question/id #uuid "4b8266ba-b293-541f-9e4f-5005a6184816" :question/math-academy-id "e-9566" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $x^2+y^2 = 2.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)  \n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}\n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\sqrt{2} \\cos   t \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\sin   t\\end{array} \\right]},\\qquad\nt \\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 1 \\\\ - 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 1 \\\\ - 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\sqrt{2} \\sin   t \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\cos   t\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\n {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\sqrt{2} \\cos   t \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\sin   t\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2}\\sin t)^2 + (\\sqrt{2}\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos{t}$ and $y(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\sin{t},$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105692 :question/id #uuid "ad8da3b7-7e57-58bb-a0d2-8cdbddf80239" :question/math-academy-id "e-9571" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf r(t) =  12\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 9\\cos t \\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi).$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) \n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}\n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}12 \\sin   t \\\\ 9 \\cos   t\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}12 \\cos   t \\\\ - 9 \\sin   t\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\n {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}9 \\sin   t \\\\ 12 \\cos   t\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{\\sqrt{144{cos}^{2}⁡t+81{sin}^{2}⁡t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0acdd021-7840-54cb-948b-ea5f4720bcd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445264} {:answer/id #uuid "554fb461-f8fc-5f02-8c53-5d662d712545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445270} {:answer/id #uuid "7c37312e-1d93-53a0-ba9c-7e273739a5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445274} {:answer/id #uuid "acdafd0f-04a3-50f3-9016-229636fea9cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445277} {:answer/id #uuid "e4176979-2302-53d4-9836-168bdc17d4a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acdafd0f-04a3-50f3-9016-229636fea9cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445277} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cfca16d-0fcb-5667-80fc-4f868f06662a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6da7c1a3-2c67-58ef-8929-9aec32acf858" :question/math-academy-id "q-126823" :question/problem "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106041 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27b161e3-83b3-5a76-83f2-9222abe12e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260221} {:answer/id #uuid "5973d8b2-fda4-5ec2-b337-e19a97cac248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260639} {:answer/id #uuid "816b713a-43d3-56d4-b4a4-da47f4e9e9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261003} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab22dbe-ff7e-5863-9ce5-5a23defe8349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261084} {:answer/id #uuid "dae04860-5c2e-5d05-af56-0252e252246b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "816b713a-43d3-56d4-b4a4-da47f4e9e9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261003} :answer-field/id #uuid "b66596a9-fd9a-5898-bd5c-c91eebb2adaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "62471caf-151d-5553-a12b-9dfaa035554e" :question/math-academy-id "q-126826" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step $2.$\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99f7b9ab-2895-5c57-9c75-8d6b8297740f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3x \\\\ 3y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102139} {:answer/id #uuid "a9636677-7c14-57f1-910d-66fb8733aea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102140} {:answer/id #uuid "dc408540-0c01-5568-aa45-f522fe602c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}y \\\\ -x\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102141} {:answer/id #uuid "df16b44e-806d-5830-b5ec-407e2468a418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102142} {:answer/id #uuid "77677902-7c56-5ae8-b1d9-1262045afd8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102143}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b1eca71e-3deb-504b-9569-e86615218d4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102138} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fca3eaa1-a46e-5f10-b465-7a2220b30d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3x \\\\ 3y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387728} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9735c9-78cb-54a4-a13e-f5ca8cff2938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387729} {:answer/id #uuid "d3afbba3-cfd4-5213-9e57-ab800cf1afbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387730} {:answer/id #uuid "95b25916-d342-5451-85a5-0b2a1bdfd639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}y \\\\ -x\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387731} {:answer/id #uuid "0adf56d6-e77b-510f-b350-053cac7c2388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0adf56d6-e77b-510f-b350-053cac7c2388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387732} :answer-field/id #uuid "1751f1fe-03fe-5ebd-b2a0-3c1b8ca3cda5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6ad4fbe5-37f0-5999-9ae5-70bbcca8a444" :question/math-academy-id "q-126830" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=3,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bde73f4-acbe-5a30-bd8d-32583815fc67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445272} {:answer/id #uuid "8a98e20c-8619-5648-a5cf-3ffe0e3b9b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445276} {:answer/id #uuid "da1537f7-60f2-5487-98fb-d8eb37445eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445282} {:answer/id #uuid "e881b58b-f16e-577e-bb7a-de715dbd1a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445284} {:answer/id #uuid "fd95b973-cef8-59e2-935c-7fcafbf777f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd95b973-cef8-59e2-935c-7fcafbf777f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445287} :answer-field/id #uuid "f63e8f91-bb3c-5e84-a629-3f6dde0dc57a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "591204f9-fa5e-50e9-8549-d7f078fe4715" :question/math-academy-id "q-126931" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262665 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48eae976-c395-5877-8e8e-2f796db2e4d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260506} {:answer/id #uuid "55869747-084d-5418-bde2-75f0b92a6c1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260605} {:answer/id #uuid "74641c30-21fb-5b05-9c60-4a34d1df9c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260893} {:answer/id #uuid "84c4d5d4-facd-5dd4-8f22-4f727860eef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.