matrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=5,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262668 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "596cbbb4-d70d-5dd7-ad56-9f0300e38315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}y \\\\ -x\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260638} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca8d8ea-aeb4-5d0b-b7d0-f35057276bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261103} {:answer/id #uuid "8da2a8e9-2823-5175-a450-ba54852bd882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261116} {:answer/id #uuid "a28b5823-d545-5e21-bc9a-c30fea55642f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}x \\\\ \\sqrt{3}y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261292} {:answer/id #uuid "db8d93ae-3247-53b6-9487-a2765d256ffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}-x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8da2a8e9-2823-5175-a450-ba54852bd882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261116} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a5a5f2f-f4f0-5629-aa38-d90ee8ce3f11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "31641955-9cb7-5a1f-9e1c-4d0cc1ef8d04" :question/math-academy-id "q-126921" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{3},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262669 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18754f51-d5fa-5357-82b0-3c1d5051df84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260091} {:answer/id #uuid "19618919-ec20-5f6a-b8e6-ceca2ed45a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260096} {:answer/id #uuid "adb5f53e-1600-55c5-9f84-4bd0bc6ce667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5}x \\\\ \\sqrt{5}y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261401} {:answer/id #uuid "e79d6159-da22-50de-aeda-fee625ef643f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261905} {:answer/id #uuid "fa122167-cfb0-5c70-b081-63ea191e612d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type