inally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262672 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0721bcfa-8892-549b-984d-222fb99e7ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259943} {:answer/id #uuid "165109fa-9646-5832-91dc-b2d298b30594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260070} {:answer/id #uuid "3303faf2-165d-5c8e-b5bd-c3bfd32f894a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260336} {:answer/id #uuid "69ccf2b8-fd52-5b36-8730-37025b8f7190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260802} {:answer/id #uuid "ecbe2583-91ae-5c48-b676-5c01aec7164a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "165109fa-9646-5832-91dc-b2d298b30594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260070} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ecdbbac-6785-5836-901b-12de3b61ed04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "00a96638-c570-5b42-af43-1f32580d4cde" :question/math-academy-id "q-126927" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262673 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fefda4d-70e4-50f7-a08f-145fda47c5e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260435} {:answer/id #uuid "7440ed46-eeb3-5630-9a94-de0bbdfc268e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260891} {:answer/id #uuid "930bf73e-0dec-5679-9db1-66acb37c6113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261154} {:answer/id #uuid "9d59d4b3-aa97-55cc-9383-959c5973b7e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261236} {:answer/id #uuid "f471b9d0-7da6-5207-aaac-5ac655c24187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "930bf73e-0dec-5679-9db1-66acb37c6113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261154} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfc55272-dded-5278-8d83-67249f477fae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ef0a8f8a-b901-5230-b4ab-2316cfe77836" :question/math-academy-id "q-126929" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=15\\