y a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445292 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3065874e-f894-5c1a-a03f-26e6b2e6c08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445267} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5bdb73-6898-56ac-bbfd-188b444b01de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445271} {:answer/id #uuid "6f4d0844-3d2a-5a55-ac0a-b06e2d2a8f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445273} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc69b79-8fb8-593f-825a-4bbd5ae3ad91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445275} {:answer/id #uuid "fad75c53-910c-59f2-aa27-ab56b7a5017d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c5bdb73-6898-56ac-bbfd-188b444b01de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445271} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2487de5-11c9-540d-8192-d573aee45967" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "79ef7418-2499-5a5e-9753-d6a82e3d1195" :question/math-academy-id "q-149680" :question/problem "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07f0b6be-b8a6-509c-9eef-fddd1e4c52cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445263} {:answer/id #uuid "4e0f7628-1647-5244-8346-f685935b3b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445269} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c95c20-8a83-5b7f-a94b-48be5207dcd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445278} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f1e04e-c989-540c-945e-5530f0a9ef5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445279} {:answer/id #uuid "ebba1cbb-de04-5f96-928a-2186ab923ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5c95c20-8a83-5b7f-a94b-48be5207dcd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445278} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f82b12c-6ce8-5ff6-b513-2e1efd6f0437" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7df57c40-4642-538c-b446-11650ad2af53" :question/math-academy-id "q-126916" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522774 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40d8306d-4b33-5cb2-8dc6-cfdc042ca82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186