\begin{bmatrix}11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522782 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2881a3fe-3c53-5f50-b217-d4f72fd2ff75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522112} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c3f5a8-5b5d-5fc0-b695-ef9b93941451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/