gent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "341cebc5-0086-51c4-a7a7-568caded3433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522137} {:answer/id #uuid "38beef69-795f-5e94-844c-2d988f85a1fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522142} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9b4350-82ac-5374-a266-911f01cdc03d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522187} {:answer/id #uuid "c6087914-80dd-50e5-8ee5-7f3d56af6f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522432} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9fc8b3-618d-5488-9e40-7e984f38b54b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd9fc8b3-618d-5488-9e40-7e984f38b54b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522483} :answer-field/id #uuid "84d37eb1-f3c7-5886-b6e4-e84343d1696f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e9ac4e11-4390-5ef8-897e-a450b29b74cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-149679" :question/problem "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522803 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a45c74-9b1e-5441-8919-df0ab8e1e247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522021} {:answer/id #uuid "08312884-c6ea-53f3-93c8-ff127f67b413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522031} {:answer/id #uuid "0e5a8fb6-b599-5596-9b2f-c97544575c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522047} {:answer/id #uuid "426a15c7-aeeb-5693-ad27-b576c0204873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522163} {:ans